# Client-Centered Coding Standards

## Core Principles

- Implement mobile-first responsive design for venue owners
- Focus on features that directly support client acquisition
- Prioritize portfolio showcase functionality
- Ensure 4-hour response system is technically supported
- Organize code by client type and venue category
- Follow WordPress coding standards for PHP development
- Maintain comprehensive documentation with client focus
- Ensure security best practices
- Optimize for performance on mobile devices
- Follow SOLID principles

## Code Organization

### File Structure
- Break large files into smaller modules (<100 lines)
- Use descriptive file names following UcF conventions
- Organize by client type or venue category
- Maintain clear separation of concerns
- Implement modular architecture

### Naming Conventions
- Variables: camelCase
- Classes: PascalCase
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE
- Functions: snake_case (WordPress standard)
- Files: kebab-case
- Follow UcF naming pattern: `ucf-[department].[function]-[description]-[date].[extension]`

### Client Type Organization
- Use directory structure reflecting venue types:
  - club/
  - lounge/
  - bar/
  - residential/
- Implement consistent patterns across venue types
- Use shared components with venue-specific customization

## Development Patterns

### WordPress Development
- Use proper hook naming conventions
- Implement nonce verification
- Sanitize inputs and escape outputs
- Use prepared statements for queries
- Follow WordPress.org plugin guidelines
- Implement proper error handling

### Portfolio Showcase Development
- Optimize image loading for mobile
- Implement lazy loading for gallery images
- Create venue-specific gallery templates
- Implement before/after comparison functionality
- Support multiple media types (images, video, 3D)
- Ensure proper taxonomy for venue categorization

### Client Contact System
- Implement immediate notification for new inquiries
- Create timestamp system for 4-hour response tracking
- Develop venue-specific contact forms
- Build automated follow-up system
- Create client interaction history
- Implement secure messaging system

## Security Standards

### Input Validation
- Validate all user inputs
- Implement proper sanitization
- Use prepared statements
- Verify nonce tokens
- Check user capabilities
- Implement rate limiting

### Output Escaping
- Escape all output appropriately
- Use context-specific escaping
- Implement XSS prevention
- Follow OWASP guidelines
- Document security measures

## Performance Optimization

### Mobile-First Optimization
- Implement responsive breakpoints prioritizing mobile
- Optimize image loading and compression
- Minimize HTTP requests
- Implement critical CSS loading
- Use performance budgets for mobile loading
- Test on actual mobile devices

### Asset Management
- Optimize images for venue portfolios
- Implement proper caching
- Use asset versioning
- Minimize HTTP requests
- Follow loading best practices

## Documentation Requirements

### Code Documentation
- Use PHPDoc standards
- Document all functions
- Explain complex logic
- Include usage examples
- Document security considerations
- Maintain changelog

### Client-Centered Documentation
- Create venue-specific implementation guides
- Document successful portfolio templates
- Maintain client interaction case studies
- Document conversion patterns by venue type
- Create client response templates

## Testing Standards

### Testing Priorities
- Mobile responsiveness testing
- Contact form functionality
- Portfolio display performance
- 4-hour response system reliability
- Client tracking accuracy
- Cross-browser compatibility

### Mobile Testing
- Test on actual mobile devices
- Verify touch interaction
- Test portfolio gallery usability
- Ensure contact forms work on small screens
- Validate performance on mobile networks
- Test client communication display on mobile

## Client-Centered Error Handling

### User-Facing Errors
- Provide clear, non-technical error messages
- Implement graceful fallbacks for all features
- Guide users through error recovery
- Collect error information for improvement
- Ensure errors don't prevent contact form submission

### Server-Side Errors
- Implement comprehensive logging
- Create immediate alerts for contact form failures
- Provide automatic recovery options
- Implement redundant contact mechanisms
- Ensure data persistence during errors

## Accessibility Standards

### Client Accessibility
- Follow WCAG 2.1 guidelines
- Implement proper ARIA roles
- Maintain keyboard navigation
- Provide alternative text for portfolio images
- Test with screen readers
- Document accessibility features

